<onlyinclude>'''Connectivity''' in graph theory concerns whether vertices can be reached from one another along paths and how robust those connections are to removal of vertices or edges.

== Explanation ==
A road network is connected if every place can be reached from every other along its roads, assuming two-way travel. A bridge that is the only connection between two parts can disconnect the graph if removed. Directed networks require additional distinctions because a route may work in only one direction.

